					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/nederbird" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2854162); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=2854162&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606273796" alt="Nederbird" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=2854162)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/nederbird" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2854162); return false;" >Nederbird</a></span> <span class="odate time_1528992858 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">14 Jun 2018 16:14</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-3835760">
									

<p>As somebody who has a few close friends with PTSD (both classic and complex), and who has seen the effect it has on them up close, the thought of an contagious form of it with vectors actively seeking to spread it is, to me, extremely disconcerting. It's also why I really liked this article. +1!</p>
<p>That aside, from the article itself, I can't really see how this is tied to Sarkicisim. I get the reference to -610 and your headcanon, but the first is (unfortunately, IMO) not regarded as canonically Sarkic, while the second usually doesn't merit tags. Don't get me wrong, I don't mind more Sarkic material, I just believe it needs something more substantial to go be tagged as &quot;sarkic&quot;. Maybe some local (Pashto?) oral legend or other narrative about Sarkic activity in the area long ago, steeped in myth yet somehow tied to the keloid satyr. Just a thought.</p>

							</div>

								
					
