														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4064900">
									

<p>Yes, PTSD is something like an overload. Or, maybe better explained, a short-circuit. It isn't natural, and it isn't more real. It isn't even heightened senses. Fear causes heightened senses.</p>
<p>PTSD kind of freezes a part of the brain on the exact events that caused the trauma. The patient's constant anxiety isn't based on their current circumstances and the &quot;sensation of still being there&quot; actually gets in the way of adaptation to new circumstances. Includes delusions and hallucinations of some degree, and even patients that fully acknowledge it isn't real have a hard time functioning under such level of anxiety, or even directing such anxiety in a new way.</p>

							</div>

								
					
